[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-80267"] :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-6$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-6,6]$\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/760595b548b2327b9c7c44f336d2c0c2b6a69688c887587f162f8ff2ac5bb5ab.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-86898/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "6e6a3b7d-4cdc-5f1a-921c-bcf2726f13a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86898/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b6b50982-951f-5bfb-b583-6c22bae6ad96" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86898/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "882891b9-d8b1-5881-b386-465e0094e042" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86898/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "5885f77f-1c1d-5617-ac43-f56402a4c8b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86898/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "882b6ae6-35dc-576a-b270-e6c30d8dc303" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86898/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "89264532-428b-52e8-a27f-a6938b52ab30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86898/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-86898/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-86898/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-86898/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-86898/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-86898/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86898" :question/id #uuid "b1caccc0-43ad-5e33-9901-fea327fe1e88" :question/math-academy-id "q-86898" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-86898/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86898"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-129783/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7b96f3f4-3890-548f-bc99-28006851bb2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129783/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5f202056-f0dd-5c9f-8d3c-c9a01c3abc1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129783/selection/5b26512916f768e66