sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III are equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-129898/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-129898"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-129909/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "acd3ce3c-c2e9-5089-ace4-d3e77d41878e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129909/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "bf89d997-8de3-5bd0-ba66-3a4d738d9124" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129909/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f