aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/414a5c8c14d70ff08d607c2a755f565c147b8b5e691a8d38a9e7db5697ba9e48.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-129909/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-129909"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
